From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:35:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] luma: one glyph size and one hover box for the shelf

An application's or an extension's indicator in the Well was drawn at its
own size and in its own colours beside system indicators drawn at 14px, so
a tray logo sat in the row twice as heavy as the glyphs next to it.

Every glyph in the row is now the system indicators' own 14px, in the
surface's ink: the theme's symbolic variant of the icon where there is one,
and otherwise the icon's own shape, holes and all, through the silhouette
the Well's own items already use. No full-colour logos in this row (owner,
2026-09-17). Luma's own Well items take the same size, so the row reads as
one row.

An icon with no transparency has no shape, so its silhouette is a filled
block. It is drawn in ink all the same: a colour a third party chose is
outside the ink contract and can land on the surface's own luminance, where
an icon shadow cannot save it -- measured at 1.00:1 on the opaque
treatments, which carry no icon shadow at all, and 1.03:1 to 1.24:1 on the
smoked ones. A block of ink is always visible, and no information is lost
because there was none. The Shell says so once in the journal, naming the
application.

The hover and pressed states along the shelf were the contents' own box, so
the highlight ran up against the glyph it covered. The Well's items, an
extension's indicator, the clock and Quick Options now share one box: 28
high, radius 8, with 7px of air around a lone glyph and 8px beside text,
inset 3px inside the Well's recess and 4px inside the island. The hit target
keeps its full height; only the highlight is inset. Pressed is that box in
the pressed fill. The dock's tiles keep their own language, where the
artwork itself lifts and no fill is drawn behind it.

Every fill is the ink ledger's hover or press for the appearance mode (Shell
patch 0150), in place of the older per-widget fills. No ink, shadow, veil or
recess value changes here. The one state that composites two fills -- Quick
Options with its menu open and the pointer on it, since the cluster's hover
wash is an actor over the button's own fill -- is added to the ledger gate's
stacks, where it holds at 4.62:1 for secondary ink against 4.5.

Focus on the shelf is drawn only while someone navigates by keyboard, the
way :focus-visible works on the web (lumaFocusVisible.js). St marks a widget
:focus whenever it holds key focus, and a menu hands key focus back to the
button it came from when it closes, so after a click on Quick Options, the
calendar or a menu the status island stayed ringed in pale blue, and a dock
icon kept a blue glow around its artwork, as if selected. Key focus itself is
unchanged, so the keyboard path works as before; inside the shelf the :focus
pseudo-class is drawn after a navigation key and cleared by the next click or
touch. What is drawn is a 2px ring in the treatment's ink, which clears the
ledger's 3:1 for a glyph on every veil; the pale blue ring measured 1.9:1 on
light paper.

An island that is one action -- a notification on the beacon, a Live
Extension that opens its application -- now highlights edge to edge at its
own radius when that action is hovered or pressed, in the ledger's fills,
with keyboard focus as a ring on the same edge; its own buttons highlight on
top. The wash is the material's inset shadow over its veil, so the island's
layout does not move. Before, only the inner button painted, inset by the
island's padding, and on the light stylesheet (light, frost and glass) a
Live Extension hovered in rgba(255,255,255,0.92) and pressed in a blue
rgba(72,120,184,0.14) that the shelf never overrode. The now-playing island
is several controls, so it takes no island wash; its controls highlight in
the ledger's fills. The two stacks a wash under a button can present,
hover under hover and hover under press, join the ledger gate's stacks.
